Description
Goal
Set up the harness for these reports, and use it to replace the hand-run weekly field adherence checks, the weekly risk adherence report, the monthly US and NULSC Defect Adherence Reports, and the bi-weekly structure adherence report with runs that start when Jira creates that period's ticket, publishing the results to Confluence with an AI-drafted summary of the findings that a person approves and the team can train, and notifying the responsible team lead automatically. People only handle exceptions: a workflow validator that is not working, a count outside expected bounds, or a team with no known lead.

This Idea also builds the one common way to show metrics, used by every Idea that shows a metric (AUTO-106, AUTO-103, AUTO-83's AUTO-84 to AUTO-87, AUTO-290 and AUTO-27): a team map, one metric record, and one viewer where people drill down from the whole IT Org to a segment, a director, a manager and a team. It is built here because this is the first Idea built.

How a run starts
A Jira automation rule creates the ticket for each period on a schedule (OSC-2855 / OS-9789 weekly, OSC-2856 / OS-9790 monthly defect, OS-9724 bi-weekly structure, and the weekly risk ticket, still to be identified). The harness polls Jira for new tickets of those kinds, and a new ticket starts the matching run. The schedule is the automation rule's schedule, so it is changed in Jira and needs no code change. The ticket is also where the run is logged: every step, decision and result is a comment on it. A ticket created by hand starts an extra run, for a re-run or a one-off. Polling is the working assumption; Phase 0 validates it.

Common metrics display
Every Idea that shows a metric does it the same way, designed once here.

One team map (Phase 7). Built from the TDIR project: each team's manager, director and segment, and a mapping from Jira projects, repositories, systems and spaces to a team. Anything unmapped is shown as Unassigned.
One metric record (designed in Phase 0, emitted by the harness from Phase 1). Each value has a metric id, the team, the period, a numerator, a denominator, a count, its source and when it was obtained, and the definition version. There is no field for a person. Counts add up, rates are the sum of numerators over the sum of denominators, and a median is computed from the underlying samples, so a rollup never averages averages.
One viewer (Phase 8). Five levels of drill-down: IT Org, segment, director, manager and team. It shows the names of managers, directors and segment heads, and never an individual engineer. Everyone can see all levels, and all team sizes are shown. A group with a missing team says 'n of m teams reporting'. Groups are never ranked.
Each report keeps its Confluence page for its detail (the findings, the issue keys), and links to the viewer for its figures.
