Savings
About 278 hours a year, from the phase estimates: the weekly check 52 hours (about an hour a week), the monthly defect report 96 hours, the bi-weekly structure report 26 hours, the summary 52 hours (the roughly hour a week the team spends writing summaries by hand with AI today, Eric's estimate), and the weekly risk report 52 hours (about an hour a week, Eric's estimate). Reviewing and approving each draft takes some of the summary's time, so its net saving is lower. The team map and the viewer (Phases 7 and 8) carry no savings; their value is that every metric across the Ideas is shown one way and can be drilled into. The catalog's own estimate for the earlier reports is lower than the phase figures: 90 tickets at 1.0 h and 25 tickets at 1.5 h, about 127 hours a year, with 10% remaining, as judgment estimates; Jira Time Spent is empty. Part of the saving is the weekly validator diagnosis, which stays human. Against a build of 242 engineer hours (605 Odd Squad hours), the saving pays back in about ten months on engineer hours and about two years and two months on Odd Squad hours.

Risks
The procedures are in SharePoint documents that have not been read; undiscovered steps may exist.
Building the harness is the largest single piece of work, and it is built before any report is useful; the first parallel run is the first proof that it works.
If a Jira automation rule does not fire, no ticket is created, a report does not run, and nothing says so; a missed ticket needs to be noticed (Q11).
Polling is an assumption. If the service identity cannot search for new tickets, or the network blocks the host from reaching Jira, Phase 4 changes shape (Q12).
A ticket created by mistake, or edited after creation, can start a run for the wrong period; the period is taken from configuration, not from free text in the ticket.
Overlap with PAR rules and with the Risk & Opportunities audit could create two definitions of the same check.
The weekly risk report lists owner names on a page that is readable by everyone who can read the Confluence instance (Eric's decision).
Migration of OSC/OS to Atlassian Cloud will change URLs, field IDs, filter names, the automation rules, TDIR and how new tickets are found; keep these in config.
Hosting is undecided; a run cannot depend on a laptop, and something has to be running to poll for new tickets.
Sending as the Odd Squad email address from an unattended identity needs permission on that mailbox.
Confluence pages that are readable by everyone cannot carry names, so any other report that needs individual names to be useful would need an exception; Phase 0 checks whether any does.
Confluence Cloud has a different interface and authentication from the Confluence the other automation uses, so it needs its own helper and service identity.
A fluent AI draft can sound more certain than the figures allow; the number check, the no-cause rule and the person who approves are the controls.
The summary waits for someone to run the approval transition each period; until then the page carries figures only. The period tickets are in more than one project and instance, each with its own workflow to change.
A guidance file that grows long or contradicts itself will give uneven drafts; it needs periodic review.
History for the summary comes only from stored records, so the first periods have no trend.
The build leans on the experts' knowledge of how the reports are produced today; their time is not in the estimates.
All team sizes are shown, so for a team of one or two the team value is in effect one person's value, which sits uneasily with the rule that metrics are never per person. The record has no person field, but the number can still identify someone.
Names of managers, directors and segment heads are visible to everyone who can use the viewer (Eric's decision).
TDIR may be incomplete or stale, and segment may not be a field in it; the viewer is only as good as the team map.
The viewer platform may limit how much data it can hold or how it can be refreshed from an unattended run.
Datadog's DORA views may differ from the viewer's and give two answers.
The common display adds work to the first Idea built, before any of the other Ideas benefit from it.
